Make fcntl(2) fail with EBADF on a descriptor that is not open.
The emulated F_GETFD, F_SETFD, F_GETFL and F_SETFL commands returned 0
for any descriptor, open or not. printf checks fcntl(1, F_GETFL) to
refuse to run with standard output closed, so "printf hi >&-" in the
shell went on to write into the closed descriptor and exited with
status 0.

Check the descriptor with _get_osfhandle() and fail with EBADF when it
has no handle, as fcntl does on POSIX systems. The other callers either
pass descriptors they have just opened or ignore the result.

---
 win32/mingw.c |    5 ++++-
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/win32/mingw.c
+++ b/win32/mingw.c
@@ -2008,7 +2008,10 @@
 		 *    fcntl(fd, F_SETFL, ret|something);
 		 * because F_SETFL isn't supported either.
 		 */
-		result = 0;
+		if ((HANDLE)_get_osfhandle(fd) == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE)
+			errno = EBADF;
+		else
+			result = 0;
 		break;
 	case F_DUPFD:
 		target = va_arg(arg, int);
